Therefore (3.20) yields:

\[\int_{\Omega}q\operatorname{div}\mathbf{v}\,dx = 2h\sum_{i=-(n-1)}^{n-1}\sum_{j=-n}^{n-1}\left\{v(2ih,(2j+1)h)-v(2ih ,2jh)\right\}\] \[= h\sum_{i=-(n-1)}^{n-1}\left\{\sum_{j=-n}^{n-1}\int_{2jh}^{(2j+1)h }\partial v(2ih,x_{2})/\partial x_{2}\,dx_{2}\right.\] \[\left.-\sum_{j=-n+1}^{n}\int_{(2j-1)h}^{2jh}\partial v(2ih,x_{2})/ \partial x_{2}\,dx_{2}\right\}.\]

Hence

\[\left|\int_{\Omega}q\operatorname{div}\mathbf{v}\,dx\right| \leqslant h\sum_{i=-(n-1)}^{n-1}\int_{-1}^{1}\left|\partial v(2ih, x_{2})/\partial x_{2}\right|dx_{2}\] \[\leqslant \sqrt{2}h(2n-1)^{1/2}\left\{\int_{-1}^{1}\sum_{i=-(n-1)}^{n-1} \left|\partial v(2ih,x_{2})/\partial x_{2}\right|^{2}dx_{2}\right\}^{1/2}.\]

Now observe that for every affine function \(f\), the following quadrature formula holds:

\[\int_{0}^{1}f^{\,2}(x)\,dx = (1/3)\{f^{\,2}(0)+f(0)f(1)+f^{\,2}(1)\}.\] \[\geqslant (1/4)\operatorname{Max}(f^{\,2}(0),f^{\,2}(1)),\]

in view of the inequality

\[ab\leqslant(1/4)a^{2}+b^{\,2}\quad\quad\forall a,\,b\geqslant 0.\]

As a consequence

\[\sum_{i=-(n-1)}^{n-1}\left|\partial v(2ih,x_{2})/\partial x_{2}\right|^{2} \leqslant(2/h)\int_{-1}^{1}\left|\partial v(x_{1},x_{2})/\partial x_{2}\right| ^{2}dx_{1}.\]

(Here we use the fact that \(\partial v/\partial x_{2}\) is a continuous and piecewise affine function of \(x_{1}\)). Therefore,

\[\left|\int_{\Omega}q\operatorname{div}\mathbf{v}\,dx\right|\leqslant 2(1-h)^{1/2} \left|\mathbf{v}\right|_{1,\Omega}.\]

Combined with (3.31), this becomes:

\[\left|\int_{\Omega}q\operatorname{div}\mathbf{v}\,dx\right|\bigg{/}\left| \mathbf{v}\right|_{1,\Omega}\leqslant\sqrt{3}h\left\|q\right\|_{0,\Omega}.\]

Thus we have proved the following result:

